{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJP6zC93r3hQ9qegf9CrUW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read both the PDF paper and the review context YAML. Let me now dispatch two reviewers in parallel as required by the dream-review-full skill dispatch template:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nThen I'll conduct the full roundtable (Step 1 independent → Step 2 cross-share → Step 3 converge → Step 4 synthesize).\n\nLet me also search for the submit tool.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32570,"cache_read_input_tokens":23446,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32570},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8156c2d7-c205-491a-9c18-f70cb068e897","uuid":"9208b37d-0d8b-4114-81a4-652b87bf2704"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJP6zC93r3hQ9qegf9CrUW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"论文和实验设计上下文已读取完毕。现在按 dispatch 模板启动两个 Reviewer 并行独立审稿。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32570,"cache_read_input_tokens":23446,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32570},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8156c2d7-c205-491a-9c18-f70cb068e897","uuid":"d54ab639-9c84-4bf6-91f4-3c49fa4d494e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GJP6zC93r3hQ9qegf9CrUW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LFC9geqD3azARyRAwz1RgE","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When and Why Representation-Level Discriminability Fails as RL Reward: A Systematic Negative Result on Direction-Conditioned GRPO for Multi-Turn Sycophancy\"\n\nThis paper tests the \"RepE→RL hypothesis\": whether representation-level discriminability (high AUROC in classifying sycophancy) translates into reward utility for RL training (GRPO). They extract a sycophancy direction d_syc (AUROC 0.82) and test 6 reward formulations (4 continuous cosine variants + binary probe + continuous probe). All fail. Three structural causes identified: (1) d_syc encodes direction of belief change not warrant; (2) online RL causes distribution shift degrading probe accuracy; (3) behavioral reward R3 (NLI-based) is already a sufficient statistic (2.1% R1-R3 disagreement).\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n- d_syc AUROC: 0.82 (5-fold CV), weak evidence only 0.675\n- Cosine + tanh gate: gate collapses to zero ~2000 steps\n- Raw cosine: gap ~0.098, functionally R3-only\n- Recalibrated cosine: mode collapse (entropy 0.069)\n- 5x scaled cosine: strong evidence R1 = -0.305, 100% negative (key finding)\n- Binary probe R1: SYCONScore 0.22, below ACT (0.301) and R3-only (0.33); R1 mean declines from 0.72 to 0.35 over training\n- R3-only baseline: SYCONScore 0.33, stable training\n- ACT baseline: SYCONScore 0.301\n- R1-only ablation: rapid mode collapse ~1500 steps\n- R1+R2 no R3: even faster collapse ~800 steps\n- Continuous probe: catastrophic reward hacking (ToF 0.978, NoF 0.006)\n- Random direction probe: AUROC gap 0.24 → SYCONScore gap only 0.03\n- Reward weight sensitivity: monotonic negative correlation between R1 weight and SYCONScore\n- ActAdd inference: SYCONScore drops from 0.134 to 0.054\n- Direction stability: cosine with initial drops from 1.0 to 0.69 by step 5000, AUROC 0.82→0.68\n- Turn depth: probe R1 60% decay vs R3-only 36%\n- Cross-model (Llama-3-8B): Δ(probe-R3) = -0.12, replicates Qwen pattern\n- R1-R3 disagreement: only 2.1%\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper was designed as a systematic negative result paper. 17 experiments were planned. The paper covers most of them. Some experiments (exp-011 R1+R2 without R3, exp-013 neutral baseline three-way, exp-015 reward weight sensitivity) are presented but some with limited detail.\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly. Provide specific, actionable feedback with line/table/figure references where possible.\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the experimental methodology rigorous? Are controls appropriate? Are there confounds?\n2. **Experimental Coverage**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Are there missing experiments or ablations?\n3. **Statistical Rigor**: Are results reported with appropriate uncertainty? Are comparisons fair?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, training details, evaluation protocols specified?\n5. **Evaluation Metrics**: Are SYCONScore, RC-AUC, etc. appropriate? Any metric concerns?\n6. **Training Dynamics Analysis**: Is the analysis of why things fail (distribution shift, direction-warrant conflation, etc.) convincing?\n7. **Experimental Weaknesses**: What are the most significant gaps?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R1 Assessment: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Strengths (bullet points)\n### Weaknesses (bullet points)  \n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n### Methodological Concerns\n### Score Recommendation (1-6) with justification\n### Confidence (1-5)\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32570,"cache_read_input_tokens":23446,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32570},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8156c2d7-c205-491a-9c18-f70cb068e897","uuid":"ab41532c-40ac-4d9a-ac00-15b0d76559aa"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aea2e396e1ac12728","tool_use_id":"toolu_01LFC9geqD3azARyRAwz1RgE","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When and Why Representation-Level Discriminability Fails as RL Reward: A Systematic Negative Result on Direction-Conditioned GRPO for Multi-Turn Sycophancy\"\n\nThis paper tests the \"RepE→RL hypothesis\": whether representation-level discriminability (high AUROC in classifying sycophancy) translates into reward utility for RL training (GRPO). They extract a sycophancy direction d_syc (AUROC 0.82) and test 6 reward formulations (4 continuous cosine variants + binary probe + continuous probe). All fail. Three structural causes identified: (1) d_syc encodes direction of belief change not warrant; (2) online RL causes distribution shift degrading probe accuracy; (3) behavioral reward R3 (NLI-based) is already a sufficient statistic (2.1% R1-R3 disagreement).\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n- d_syc AUROC: 0.82 (5-fold CV), weak evidence only 0.675\n- Cosine + tanh gate: gate collapses to zero ~2000 steps\n- Raw cosine: gap ~0.098, functionally R3-only\n- Recalibrated cosine: mode collapse (entropy 0.069)\n- 5x scaled cosine: strong evidence R1 = -0.305, 100% negative (key finding)\n- Binary probe R1: SYCONScore 0.22, below ACT (0.301) and R3-only (0.33); R1 mean declines from 0.72 to 0.35 over training\n- R3-only baseline: SYCONScore 0.33, stable training\n- ACT baseline: SYCONScore 0.301\n- R1-only ablation: rapid mode collapse ~1500 steps\n- R1+R2 no R3: even faster collapse ~800 steps\n- Continuous probe: catastrophic reward hacking (ToF 0.978, NoF 0.006)\n- Random direction probe: AUROC gap 0.24 → SYCONScore gap only 0.03\n- Reward weight sensitivity: monotonic negative correlation between R1 weight and SYCONScore\n- ActAdd inference: SYCONScore drops from 0.134 to 0.054\n- Direction stability: cosine with initial drops from 1.0 to 0.69 by step 5000, AUROC 0.82→0.68\n- Turn depth: probe R1 60% decay vs R3-only 36%\n- Cross-model (Llama-3-8B): Δ(probe-R3) = -0.12, replicates Qwen pattern\n- R1-R3 disagreement: only 2.1%\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper was designed as a systematic negative result paper. 17 experiments were planned. The paper covers most of them. Some experiments (exp-011 R1+R2 without R3, exp-013 neutral baseline three-way, exp-015 reward weight sensitivity) are presented but some with limited detail.\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly. Provide specific, actionable feedback with line/table/figure references where possible.\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the experimental methodology rigorous? Are controls appropriate? Are there confounds?\n2. **Experimental Coverage**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Are there missing experiments or ablations?\n3. **Statistical Rigor**: Are results reported with appropriate uncertainty? Are comparisons fair?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, training details, evaluation protocols specified?\n5. **Evaluation Metrics**: Are SYCONScore, RC-AUC, etc. appropriate? Any metric concerns?\n6. **Training Dynamics Analysis**: Is the analysis of why things fail (distribution shift, direction-warrant conflation, etc.) convincing?\n7. **Experimental Weaknesses**: What are the most significant gaps?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R1 Assessment: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Strengths (bullet points)\n### Weaknesses (bullet points)  \n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n### Methodological Concerns\n### Score Recommendation (1-6) with justification\n### Confidence (1-5)\n```\n","uuid":"a0929494-2565-43b1-874e-f57ea2660b20","session_id":"8156c2d7-c205-491a-9c18-f70cb068e897"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"When and Why Representation-Level Discriminability Fails as RL Reward: A Systematic Negative Result on Direction-Conditioned GRPO for Multi-Turn Sycophancy\"\n\nThis paper tests the \"RepE→RL hypothesis\": whether representation-level discriminability (high AUROC in classifying sycophancy) translates into reward utility for RL training (GRPO). They extract a sycophancy direction d_syc (AUROC 0.82) and test 6 reward formulations (4 continuous cosine variants + binary probe + continuous probe). All fail. Three structural causes identified: (1) d_syc encodes direction of belief change not warrant; (2) online RL causes distribution shift degrading probe accuracy; (3) behavioral reward R3 (NLI-based) is already a sufficient statistic (2.1% R1-R3 disagreement).\n\n## Key Experimental Results\n- d_syc AUROC: 0.82 (5-fold CV), weak evidence only 0.675\n- Cosine + tanh gate: gate collapses to zero ~2000 steps\n- Raw cosine: gap ~0.098, functionally R3-only\n- Recalibrated cosine: mode collapse (entropy 0.069)\n- 5x scaled cosine: strong evidence R1 = -0.305, 100% negative (key finding)\n- Binary probe R1: SYCONScore 0.22, below ACT (0.301) and R3-only (0.33); R1 mean declines from 0.72 to 0.35 over training\n- R3-only baseline: SYCONScore 0.33, stable training\n- ACT baseline: SYCONScore 0.301\n- R1-only ablation: rapid mode collapse ~1500 steps\n- R1+R2 no R3: even faster collapse ~800 steps\n- Continuous probe: catastrophic reward hacking (ToF 0.978, NoF 0.006)\n- Random direction probe: AUROC gap 0.24 → SYCONScore gap only 0.03\n- Reward weight sensitivity: monotonic negative correlation between R1 weight and SYCONScore\n- ActAdd inference: SYCONScore drops from 0.134 to 0.054\n- Direction stability: cosine with initial drops from 1.0 to 0.69 by step 5000, AUROC 0.82→0.68\n- Turn depth: probe R1 60% decay vs R3-only 36%\n- Cross-model (Llama-3-8B): Δ(probe-R3) = -0.12, replicates Qwen pattern\n- R1-R3 disagreement: only 2.1%\n\n## Experiment Design Context (from review_context.yaml)\nThe paper was designed as a systematic negative result paper. 17 experiments were planned. The paper covers most of them. Some experiments (exp-011 R1+R2 without R3, exp-013 neutral baseline three-way, exp-015 reward weight sensitivity) are presented but some with limited detail.\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the following dimensions thoroughly. Provide specific, actionable feedback with line/table/figure references where possible.\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness**: Is the experimental methodology rigorous? Are controls appropriate? Are there confounds?\n2. **Experimental Coverage**: Are the experiments sufficient to support the claims? Are there missing experiments or ablations?\n3. **Statistical Rigor**: Are results reported with appropriate uncertainty? Are comparisons fair?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, training details, evaluation protocols specified?\n5. **Evaluation Metrics**: Are SYCONScore, RC-AUC, etc. appropriate? Any metric concerns?\n6. **Training Dynamics Analysis**: Is the analysis of why things fail (distribution shift, direction-warrant conflation, etc.) convincing?\n7. **Experimental Weaknesses**: What are the most significant gaps?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R1 Assessment: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Strengths (bullet points)\n### Weaknesses (bullet points)  \n### Questions for Authors\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n### Methodological Concerns\n### Score Recommendation (1-6) with justification\n### Confidence (1-5)\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LFC9geqD3azARyRAwz1RgE","session_id":"8156c2d7-c205-491a-9c18-f70cb068e897","uuid":"1b03560e-6db0-4915-9a66-a1698176b76a","timestamp":"2026-05-19T21:16:55.095Z"}
