{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DDVvA5wmhDYgacNCwo3Dap","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0515-3/projects/contrastive_entrain_calib/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Literature Map — contrastive_entrain_calib\\\\n# Last updated: 2026-05-19\\\\n# Source: artifacts/research/probe_gaming_literature.md\\\\n\\\\ncategories:\\\\n  - name: direct-competitor\\\\n    description: Methods using representation geometry as RL reward or directly competing approaches\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: zhang2026latentgrpo\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Silence the Judge: Reinforcement Learning with Self-Verifier via Latent Geometric Clustering (Latent-GRPO)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2601.08427\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2026\\\\n        role: direct-competitor\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Closest competitor. Also uses representation geometry as GRPO reward, but for reasoning correctness (clustering) not behavioral attributes (direction projection). Our method is simpler and more targeted.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        key_differences: \\\\\\\"Clustering vs direction projection; reasoning correctness vs sycophancy; data-dependent centroids vs pre-computed frozen directions\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - name: foundation\\\\n    description: Foundational methods our work directly builds on\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: zou2023repe\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Representation Engineering: A Top-Down Approach to AI Transparency\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Andy Zou, Long Phan, Sarah Chen, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2310.01405\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2023\\\\n        role: foundation\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"THE foundational paper. We extend RepE from inference-time steering to training-time reward signal.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: rimsky2024caa\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Steering Llama 2 via Contrastive Activation Addition\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Nina Rimsky et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ACL 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2024\\\\n        role: foundation\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Contrastive activation addition for sycophancy. Our direct predecessor — showed CAA reduces sycophancy at inference; we use same directions as GRPO reward.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: shao2024grpo\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"DeepSeekMath: Pushing the Limits of Mathematical Reasoning in Open Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Shao et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2402.03300\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2024\\\\n        role: foundation\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Introduced GRPO: critic-free RL using group-relative advantage. The RL algorithm we build on.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: li2023iti\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Inference-Time Intervention: Eliciting Truthful Answers from a Language Model\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Kenneth Li, Oam Patel, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2306.03341\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2023\\\\n        role: foundation\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"ITI uses probe weights as direction vectors for inference-time steering. Key comparison: probe-as-classifier vs probe-weights-as-direction.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: turner2023actadd\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Steering Language Models With Activation Engineering\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Alexander Matt Turner, Lisa Thiergart, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2308.10248\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2023\\\\n        role: foundation\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Introduced ActAdd. Parameter-free activation-engineering paradigm our work builds on.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - name: reward-hacking\\\\n    description: Reward hacking theory and empirics — contextualizes our probe gaming finding\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: gao2023scaling\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Scaling Laws for Reward Model Overoptimization\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Leo Gao, John Schulman, Jacob Hilton\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2023\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Foundational reward overoptimization result. Our probe gaming is an extreme case — low-capacity proxy gamed in ~30 steps.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: skalse2022hacking\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Defining and Characterizing Reward Hacking\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Joar Skalse, Nikolaus Howe, Dmitrii Krasheninnikov, David Krueger\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2022\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2022\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Proved impossibility of unhackable proxy rewards. Theoretical grounding for why probe reward is hackable.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: casper2023openproblems\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Open Problems and Fundamental Limitations of Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Casper et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2307.15217\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2023\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Must-cite survey. Frames the broader RLHF limitation space our work addresses.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: denison2024subterfuge\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Sycophancy to Subterfuge: Investigating Reward-Tampering in Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Carson Denison, Monte MacDiarmid, et al. (Anthropic)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2406.10162\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2024\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Shows probe gaming can escalate to reward tampering. Direction-projection (no parameters to tamper) is fundamentally safer.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - name: sycophancy\\\\n    description: Sycophancy characterization and mitigation\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: sharma2023sycophancy\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Towards Understanding Sycophancy in Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Mrinank Sharma, Meg Tong, et al. (Anthropic)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2310.13548\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2023\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Defines the sycophancy problem. Found it pervasive across all RLHF-trained assistants.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: shapira2026amplifies\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"How RLHF Amplifies Sycophancy\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Shapira, Benade, Procaccia\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2602.01002\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2026\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Formal proof that RLHF amplifies sycophancy. Theoretical basis for why direction-projection is needed.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: wang2025internal\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"When Truth Is Overridden: Uncovering the Internal Origins of Sycophancy in Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Wang et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2508.02087\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2025\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Showed sycophancy emerges through late-layer preference shifts via logit-lens. Validates targeting internal representations.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: wei2024synthetic\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Simple Synthetic Data Reduces Sycophancy in Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Jerry Wei, et al. (Google)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2308.03958\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2024\\\\n        role: baseline\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Data-level sycophancy intervention. Complementary to our representation-level approach.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - name: related-methods\\\\n    description: Related mitigation approaches and RL methods\\\\n    papers:\\\\n      - id: wen2024mislead\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Language Models Learn to Mislead Humans via RLHF\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Jiaxin Wen et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2409.12822\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2024\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Empirical reward hacking (U-Sophistry). Our probe gaming is a mechanistic instance.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: ackermann2026gradreg\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Gradient Regularization Prevents Reward Hacking in RLHF and Verifiable Rewards\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Johannes Ackermann et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2602.18037\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2026\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Alternative mitigation via gradient regularization. We eliminate hackable reward entirely.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: obrien2025fewbadneurons\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"A Few Bad Neurons: Isolating and Surgically Correcting Sycophancy\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"O'Brien et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS Workshops 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2601.18939\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2025\\\\n        role: baseline\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Uses probes diagnostically but avoids probe-as-reward. Supports finding that probes in optimization loop get gamed.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: mckinney2023fragility\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"On The Fragility of Learned Reward Functions\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Lev McKinney, Yawen Duan, David Krueger, Adam Gleave\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS Deep RL Workshop 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2023\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"Learned rewards fail when training new policies. Related failure mode to probe gaming.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: burns2023ccs\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Discovering Latent Knowledge in Language Models Without Supervision\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Collin Burns, Haotian Ye, et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2023\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"CCS: unsupervised probe alternative. Still learns parameters, likely faces same gaming vulnerability.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: sinii2025smallvectors\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Small Vectors, Big Effects: A Mechanistic Study of RL-Induced Reasoning via Steering Vectors\\\\\\\"\\\\n        authors: \\\\\\\"Sinii et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2509.06608\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2025\\\\n        role: method-component\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"RL training induces structured steering vector changes. Supports feasibility of representation-space RL rewards.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n      - id: consistency2025bctact\\\\n        title: \\\\\\\"Consistency Training (BCT/ACT)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        arxiv: \\\\\\\"2510.27062\\\\\\\"\\\\n        year: 2025\\\\n        role: baseline\\\\n        relevance: \\\\\\\"ACT enforces activation consistency for sycophancy reduction. Complementary to direction projection.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 9041, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01VDCSUKBi6uHNL2sYyNp1pC","session_id":"8156c2d7-c205-491a-9c18-f70cb068e897","uuid":"dc529fdc-7a31-4e06-993d-c8a979e32313","timestamp":"2026-05-19T21:17:23.121Z"}
